entry beta-decay
term: Beta Decay
related_terms: beta-particle
related_terms: decay
related_terms: radioactivity
related_terms: neutron
related_terms: proton
related_terms: electron
quick_facts: A beta particle is a fast electron
quick_facts: A neutron changes into a proton and an electron is emitted
quick_facts: The electron was not orbiting the nucleus before
quick_facts: A tiny antineutrino comes out too
quick_facts: The number of protons goes up by one, so the element changes

sources:
    {"organization":"OpenStax","title":"University Physics Volume 3: Radioactive Decay","url":"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-3-radioactive-decay"}

definition:
    A kind of radioactive decay in which a nucleus gives off a beta particle, a fast electron. In the usual kind, beta-minus decay, a neutron in the nucleus changes into a proton and an electron is emitted, along with a tiny particle called an antineutrino. The number of protons goes up by one, so the nucleus becomes a different element.

debunking_snippet:
    “The beta electron was one of the atom's orbiting electrons.” It is made in the decay itself, when a neutron changes into a proton.
